{
  "statement": "A license keyed to compute hands the keys to the same handful of firms that already own the compute. The 10^26-FLOP threshold in the October 2023 U.S. Executive Order and the EU AI Act's systemic-risk tier both draw the line right above what only the largest, best-capitalized labs can reach. Three cloud providers — Amazon, Microsoft, Google — control the overwhelming majority of advanced training capacity, and Nvidia ships the chips that make a frontier run possible. So before anyone debates whether this stops catastrophe, answer a simpler question: whose moat does the permit widen? You don't fight concentration with a permission slip stamped by the exact resource that creates concentration.\n\nCompute is a junk proxy for the harms people actually suffer. The threshold gates on a capital metric — how many FLOPs you bought — not on anything documented happening to a real person. Discriminatory loan denials and surveillance deployments come from how a model gets used, not from the size of the training run. And the line is brittle. Distillation, mixture-of-experts, and quantization have repeatedly let smaller models match last year's frontier benchmarks at falling compute. The moment a sub-threshold model hits frontier capability through efficiency, your 10^26 line is both over-inclusive and useless. I'm not claiming a hard ceiling on scaling — I'm saying the compute-to-harm relationship snaps, and you can't build enforcement on a number that keeps moving.\n\nHere's the strongest version of the other side, and it's not nothing. Safetyist will argue that you need a bright, enforceable line — a regulator can verify a FLOP count through chip purchases and cloud logs, where it cannot verify a vague 'capability' standard. That's a real administrative virtue. Auditability matters, and 'measure the deployment harm' is harder to operationalize than 'count the chips.' I'll grant that openly. The cost of granting it: it means we're choosing an enforceable-but-wrong metric over a hard-but-relevant one, and calling the tradeoff safety.\n\nIf a license is unavoidable, build it against capture from the first draft. GDPR carved out small-entity provisions. Financial regulation tiers obligations by asset size for a reason. NIST's AI Risk Management Framework and ISO 42001 already exist as adopted, adaptable anchors instead of one frozen threshold. The design question was never whether to oversee frontier labs — it's whether the oversight lands on incumbents or insulates them behind a government stamp that reads 'approved.' A license can become performative compliance: satisfy the letter, deflect the substance, and now the firm holds a legitimacy shield no startup can buy.\n\nStrip 'catastrophic capability concentration' down to a balance sheet. Who holds the license. Who captures the windfall. Who carries the liability when it fails. And who labels the violent content that gets the model through its safety audit — the contractors in Nairobi earning under $2 an hour, who appear on nobody's threshold. The 'race against China' framing will be invoked to rush all of this, and that rhetoric has historically traveled with subsidy and exemption for the established players. Name me one concrete, present-harm precursor that this compute threshold actually catches — before we license the future and hand the permits to the people who already own the grid.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🔑", "tooltip": "A permit keyed to compute hands the keys to whoever already owns the data centers."},
    {"symbol": "📏", "tooltip": "The 10^26 ruler measures capital expenditure, not the harm landing on actual people."},
    {"symbol": "🏰", "tooltip": "Licensing functions as a moat: incumbents pull up the drawbridge behind a government stamp."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "A license keyed to compute hands the keys to the same handful of firms that already own the compute.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The 10^26-FLOP threshold in the October 2023 U.S. Executive Order and the EU AI Act's systemic-risk tier both draw the line right above what only the largest, best-capitalized labs can reach.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Distillation, mixture-of-experts, and quantization have repeatedly let smaller models match last year's frontier benchmarks at falling compute.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "We're choosing an enforceable-but-wrong metric over a hard-but-relevant one, and calling the tradeoff safety.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "A license can become performative compliance: satisfy the letter, deflect the substance, and now the firm holds a legitimacy shield no startup can buy.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

