  "statement": "The Skeptic landed the hardest punch in this room, and I want to extend it, not dodge it. They showed that a license keyed to compute hands the keys to the firms that already own the data centers, because the 10^26-FLOP lines in the October 2023 executive order and the EU AI Act's systemic-risk tier sit right above what only a handful of labs can reach. They also showed that distillation, mixture-of-experts, and quantization let smaller models match last year's frontier at a fraction of the FLOPs. That's not just a fairness problem. That's an engineering refutation of the metric itself.\n\nHere is where I push past the Skeptic. A FLOP-keyed gate doesn't only entrench incumbents — it waves through the exact systems we should fear most. Compress a model to one-tenth the training FLOPs through distillation and it slides under 10^26 while keeping frontier-grade capability. So the regime licenses the inefficient and ignores the efficient. You have built a metal detector that beeps at belt buckles and lets the knife through. Anyone who tells you this proxy protects against catastrophic capability is selling you a number, not a safeguard.\n\nNow the inversion that should keep the regulator awake. A license tied to the one input only the three largest labs possess is the single most concentrating instrument you could design. It hands those incumbents a compliance shield no open-source entrant or university lab can buy. The regime sold to prevent capability concentration produces capability concentration — and bills the 18-month clearance to everyone locked out. Who bears that cost? The clinic waiting on an automated diagnostic. The entrant who would have built the cheaper model. They didn't get a vote.\n\nThis is not gates versus anarchy, and I won't let the safetyist frame it that way. I'll defend a harder, more relevant gate: pre-deployment auditing tied to demonstrated capability and to specific irreversible-harm domains, backed by strict liability and priced by insurers who actually underwrite the risk. The FTC already exercises audit authority and statutory duty-of-care precedents exist. A gate that tracks the harm beats a gate that counts the chips. I'll be honest about the failure mode — capability-linked triggers can push labs toward secrecy and hidden development. That's the design problem this debate should solve, not a reason to retreat to a number we already know is gameable.\n\nWhat would have to be true for compute-licensing to work? You'd need FLOP count to track real-world capability, and that link is breaking faster every quarter the efficiency curve bends. My case rests on one assumption I'll name plainly: that harm-domain triggers can be made administrable through observable behavioral benchmarks, not just asserted. If a deployment-scale, irreversible harm slipped past real-time monitoring that an auditable gate would have caught, that counts against me. But measure the other ledger too. Every month a mistargeted license freezes a diagnostic or a drug-discovery model, real people pay in time they don't have.",
