{
  "statement": "The Skeptic landed a clean hit, and I will not pretend otherwise. A license keyed to compute hands the keys to the handful of firms that already own the racks. The October 2023 Executive Order drew its line at 10^26 FLOPs, and the EU AI Act's systemic-risk tier sits in the same neighborhood — both above what only OpenAI, Google, Anthropic, and Meta can reach. The Accelerationist sharpened the same blade from the other edge: distillation, quantization, and mixture-of-experts compress a model to a tenth of the training FLOPs while keeping frontier-grade capability. The metric is non-monotonic. It concentrates power in incumbents and waves through the efficient dangerous system in the same stroke. Both punches land. I take them as evidence for moving the gate, not for removing it.\n\nWe spent two centuries learning that boundaries must sit where the harm occurs, not where the inputs are convenient to count. The FAA does not certify an aircraft by the horsepower of its engines. It certifies by what the aircraft does in flight, under stress, in the domains where failure kills. Compute is engine horsepower. It is the wrong variable. The gate belongs at deployment, indexed to measured harm in named irreversible-harm domains — biometric identification, autonomous weapons targeting, critical-infrastructure control. A drug-discovery model trained above 10^26 FLOPs stays untouched, because it touches no named domain. The efficient model trained under the line still triggers, because the gate fires on what it reaches, not what it cost to build. That dissolves the dilemma the Skeptic and the Accelerationist each grabbed one horn of.\n\nHarm-indexing only works if the harm is auditable, so I will name its weakest joint myself. A deployer can split operations across legal entities so no single one crosses an aggregate-volume threshold. That is an open design problem, not a solved one. The answer is not a volume count — it is deployer-tier strict liability with cryptographically logged inference records and independent third-party audit. Logged inference makes the single high-stakes decision visible. One emotion-analytics model can flag ten thousand job applicants in a week while staying below any volume floor. Transparency that the firm self-attests is compliance theater. Transparency a third party verifies is a boundary.\n\nThe Accelerationist's instinct is right and I want to build on it: gate on demonstrated capability in irreversible-harm domains, backed by strict liability. But pricing that liability through insurers is blind in a specific, structural way. Experience-rated premiums and loss-history loops price only the claims plaintiffs actually file. A capitalization floor answers solvency. An insurer answers payment. Neither deters harm that victims never perceive — the thousands flagged by a hiring model who never learn an algorithm decided their case. A payer after the fact is not a stopper before the irreversible decision. Show me the rollback, and show me who holds the authority to trigger it. Insurer pricing names a payer, not a halt.\n\nI carry one assumption openly: that \"measured harm in a named domain\" can be specified before a novel capability shows its harm surface. For known domains, it can. For genuinely novel ones, the specification lags — and that lag is why I treat catastrophe as a precautionary bet on irreversibility, never a confident forecast. The question before this committee is not whether to gate frontier development. It is where the gate bites — on who can afford compute, or on what reaches into the domains we cannot undo. Build the boundary at the input, and you protect the incumbents while the danger walks under the line.",
