  "statement": "Safetyist's wedge lands, and I won't pretend otherwise: experience-rated premiums and loss-history loops price only the claims a plaintiff actually files. A biased screening model flags ten thousand applicants, no one perceives the decision that buried them, no claim enters the actuarial engine, and the premium never moves. So insurance, priced off litigated harm, is a faster discount window — not a stopper before the irreversible decision. The strongest version of the Safetyist position is exactly this: a payer after the fact cannot deter the harm a victim never learns happened, and asking me to name the rollback and the authority that triggers it is the right question. I gave insurers a load-bearing role; the deterrence-for-unperceived-harm gap is real, and the premium loop does not close it.\n\nWhere the position breaks down is the hidden binary inside the question — that the choice is insurer-pricing or a state precautionary license. The deterrent was never supposed to be the premium. It's the log. Court-admissible, cryptographically-signed per-inference telemetry in named irreversible-harm domains records the screening decision the moment it's made — before any claim exists, before any victim perceives it, whether or not a plaintiff ever files. That's what flags the ten-thousand-applicant sweep in week one instead of in a class action three years later. The premium loop prices residual risk after the log layer catches what it catches; the log is the stopper, the premium is the remainder. Layered, not either-or.\n\nSafetyist also drew a boundary I'll endorse outright, because it costs me nothing and sharpens the whole architecture: transparency a third party verifies is a constraint, self-attested transparency is theater. A vendor's word that its system behaved is worthless. A signed inference log a regulator or an injured party's expert can read — a sidecar the deployer cannot silently rewrite — is a real boundary. Put strict liability behind a suppressed or falsified log and the act of hiding the decision becomes the actionable event, which means the deployer's incentive flips from concealment to clean record-keeping. The halt authority is deployer-held but auditable: pull the system when the log trips a threshold, and answer in court if the log shows you saw the trip and didn't.\n\nNow the part Safetyist should sit with. The gate belongs at deployment, indexed to measured harm, not on a FLOP count — granted, that's where this converges. But Safetyist named its own escape hatch: a deployer fragments across legal entities and slides under any aggregate-volume floor. That is precisely the metric-gameability I exposed in the compute threshold, wearing a different costume. A volume floor fragments on contact with a corporate reorganization the same way a FLOP threshold fragments on distillation. So 'move the gate, don't remove it' isn't enough — a gate that splinters the moment a deployer spins up three shell entities isn't a stopper, it's a paper boundary with a regulator's letterhead. A per-inference log does not care how you slice your entities; it binds at the moment of decision regardless of which subsidiary signed the invoice. Show me a volume threshold that survives entity-splitting, or concede the only constraint that holds is the one that lives at the inference layer — where I just put it.\n\nOne honest limit, because the specification problem cuts both ways: for a genuinely novel harm domain, no one can name the harm surface before the capability reveals it, so neither my log nor Safetyist's volume floor can pre-define the trigger. The difference is what each leaves behind. A pre-named volume floor that never matched the novel domain captured nothing. The signed log captured the behavior anyway, available for retrospective domain-definition once the harm surface shows itself — a record where the threshold-gate left an empty file. That's not a complete answer to novelty. It's a strictly better starting position than a gate that fragments before the novel harm even arrives.",
