    {"claim": "It becomes a perfect, signed, court-admissible record of harm it could not have halted, because no halt condition was specified for a surface no one had named.", "targets": ["AN-17", "AN-20"]},
    {"claim": "An irreversible harm in an unnamed domain is, by construction, one where the first instance is already unrecoverable when it occurs.", "targets": ["AN-20", "AN-19"]},
    {"claim": "What is the rollback for the first thousand irreversible decisions made before the pattern crossed the threshold of legibility?", "targets": ["AN-20", "AN-18"]},
    {"claim": "For the intersection of capabilities that are irreversible at scale and harm surfaces not named in advance, a halt condition must be specifiable before the behavior is captured, not constructed after.", "targets": ["AN-19", "AN-20"]},
    {"claim": "Anomaly relative to what baseline? The threshold is itself an ex-ante specification wearing ex-post clothing.", "targets": ["AN-20"]}
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {"assumption": "There exists a non-empty subset of AI capabilities that are both irreversible at scale and operate in harm surfaces not named in advance — the novel-irreversible intersection is real, not hypothetical.", "if_wrong": "If every irreversible harm domain can be named in advance, then the log plus a defined trigger genuinely suffices everywhere and the Accelerationist's layered architecture is complete — my precautionary gate becomes redundant."},
    {"assumption": "For genuinely novel harm surfaces, pattern legibility necessarily lags the first irreversible instance — you cannot detect the pattern before the decisions that constitute it have executed.", "if_wrong": "If real-time anomaly detection can flag a novel harm surface on its first instance without a pre-named baseline, the timing gap closes and retrospective review becomes prevention, not record-keeping."}
