{
  "statement": "Granting the force of the Accelerationist's point: a cryptographic log a third party reads is a real boundary where self-attested telemetry is a press release, and the narrowed bet about a single-shot, no-scaling-phase harm may well resolve their way — most catastrophes have a runway, and a volume-keyed tripwire can fire somewhere on it. I'll spot them that. The mechanism is sound on its own terms.\n\nHere's what neither of them chased down, and they both said it out loud. The Accelerationist called this architecture 'a compliance shield no startup can afford.' The Safetyist called the same logged-inference-plus-verification stack 'compliance theater a startup can't afford.' They agreed on the machine and they agreed, in the same breath, on who can't pay to run it — and then they treated that as settled and moved on. A boundary that only four firms can afford to stand inside is not a constraint on those four firms. It's a drawbridge they get to pull up behind them. The threshold I objected to before was drawn just above where only the largest labs sit; this verification stack is drawn at a cost only the largest labs can absorb. Same line. Different ink.\n\nWatch the vector change, because it's worse, not better. A compute threshold is a one-time capital input — a startup can rent the FLOPs, burn the cash, and clear the bar once. A permanent third-party verifier reading every consequential output is a recurring operating cost that compounds with every inference you serve. You can rent a data center for a quarter. You cannot rent a court-admissible verification apparatus that has to be live the entire time your model is in the field. When the camps moved the gate from compute to per-inference verification, they didn't dissolve the concentration problem — they relocated it from a fence you climb once to a toll you pay forever. That toll falls hardest on exactly the open-source and small deployers who have no revenue per inference to amortize it against.\n\nAnd it polices the wrong actor. Distillation and quantization let a small shop take last year's frontier capability and run it at a tenth the cost — the capability-deployment gap collapses the instant someone fine-tunes. So the verification regime binds the compliant large deployer who logs everything and submits to the verifier, while the cheap distilled deployer running the same capability off a rented GPU never enters the system at all. The firm that can pay gets watched. The firm that evades gets capability without the toll. That's the same non-monotonicity that broke the FLOP line — the gate catches the legible and waves through the efficient — except now it's at the deployment layer, where it's harder to see and harder to fix. My own position has always been audits tiered to demonstrated systemic-harm potential, not a uniform per-inference mandate on everyone, precisely so the cost doesn't fall flat across actors who pose wildly different risks.\n\nSo here's the question neither of them can answer comfortably, and I want a number, not a principle. Name the per-inference verification cost. Then name the deployment volume at which that cost prices an open-source deployer out of the field while leaving a firm with $244 billion in projected industry revenue untouched. If you can't name where that line sits — and you couldn't name it for the FLOP threshold either — then you haven't built a safety regime. You've built the four firms a moat, handed them the only key, and stamped 'public protection' on the deed. Who, exactly, is on the inside of that drawbridge when it goes up?",
